# Shell and git commits

On Windows, run shell commands with **cmd**, not PowerShell.

```text
cmd /c "git status"
cmd /c "git add path1 path2"
```

## Git commit

Do **not** run `git commit` as a plain shell command (Cursor may inject `--trailer`, which breaks old Git). Commit via Python:

```text
cmd /c "python -c \"from pathlib import Path; import subprocess; p=Path('.git/COMMIT_EDITMSG_TMP'); p.write_text('subject\\n\\nbody\\n', encoding='utf-8'); r=subprocess.run(['git','commit','-F',str(p)]); p.unlink(missing_ok=True); raise SystemExit(r.returncode)\""
```

Or write a short `.py` under `.git/`, run it with `cmd /c python .git/commit_via_py.py`, then delete it.

## Protocol

- Only commit when asked
- Stage only intentional files (no secrets, no `.vsix` unless asked)
- Verify with `git status` / `git log -1`
- `git status`, `diff`, `log`, `add` are fine via `cmd /c`
